# macOS local execution: suspected assertion-formatting memory amplification; user reported >100 GB and forced quit

## Environment

- Date: 2026-10-13.
- macOS 27.0.1.
- Installed desktop compatibility surface: ChatGPT.app 26.1007.21159, build 20052, used for a Codex local coding session. Version read from application bundle metadata; the About dialog was not inspected.
- Local Node.js: v24.18.1; jsdom 29.1.1; React 19.2.5.
- Subscription and bundled app-server version were not collected.

## Incident and attribution limits

The user reported that memory usage exceeded 100 GB and the desktop app had to be force quit while an agent was checking a local interactive UI mockup. That peak was not independently measured by this investigation. No per-process memory recording or heap dump from the incident is available, so it is unknown whether the growing process was Node, the desktop app, or aggregate process memory. This report does not establish a leak in Codex's own process.

The last completed test logs indicated that a recipient option had been selected. The next operation was a strict Node assertion comparing a queried DOM listbox to `null`. The listbox could remain mounted because the selection handler closed the popup and then focused the input; the input's focus handler reopened the popup. The jsdom interaction harness programmatically changed input values without necessarily focusing the input first. In addition, the failure handler printed the raw Error object.

## Strongly supported initiating mechanism

A failed object-versus-null Node assertion eagerly formats the actual object. Inspection of the installed Node assertion implementation confirms `depth: 1000`, `maxArrayLength: Infinity`, `customInspect: false`, and `compact: false`. DOM nodes used by React have enumerable framework graph references; formatting them can therefore expand shared object graphs rather than a short DOM description. The unmodified private DOM/React tree was deliberately not rerun or printed.

A tiny synthetic shared-reference graph, without DOM, React, private data, or app dependencies, supports severe assertion-message amplification. Each graph level referenced the same preceding object twice. Under a 128 MiB V8 old-space limit and a 10-second external timeout, the following measurements completed successfully:

| Graph depth | Unique objects | Assertion message bytes | Elapsed ms | Observed RSS MiB |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 6 | 2,836 | 1 | 47 |
| 8 | 9 | 30,004 | 5 | 68 |
| 11 | 12 | 301,108 | 582 | 879 |

Only scalar measurements were emitted. No assertion message or object graph was emitted. The depth-11 result also establishes that `--max-old-space-size` alone is insufficient as a total RSS limit. No larger reproduction was attempted. This proves a formatter amplification mechanism, not attribution of the original >100 GB incident.

## Small sanitized reproduction

Run only the small depths shown below in a child process with an independent RSS ceiling and a hard wall-clock limit. Do not increase the depth to reproduce the reported incident. The graph contains shared aliases; it is not a binary tree of independently allocated nodes.

```js
// Node --max-old-space-size=128; external timeout <=10 seconds.
// Add independent process RSS monitoring; the V8 heap flag is not an RSS cap.
const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
for (const depth of [5, 8]) {
  let graph = { leaf: true };
  for (let i = 0; i < depth; i++) graph = { left: graph, right: graph };
  const started = performance.now();
  try {
    assert.equal(graph, null);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      depth,
      uniqueObjects: depth + 1,
      messageBytes: Buffer.byteLength(error.message),
      elapsedMs: Math.round(performance.now() - started),
      rssMiB: Math.round(process.memoryUsage().rss / 1048576),
    }));
  }
}
```

## Containment and result

The local mockup handler was corrected to focus before its final close-state update. DOM/null equality assertions were replaced with boolean-presence assertions, so a failure contains only scalar values. Error logging was changed to sanitized bounded strings rather than raw Error, DOM, or framework objects.

The full mockup check subsequently passed 19 checks in 15.9 seconds with observed peak RSS 232.1 MiB, under a 192 MiB V8 old-space setting, a monitored 512 MiB RSS ceiling, a 45-second hard timeout, and a 64 KiB output ceiling. This is evidence for successful containment of this check, not proof that every possible desktop memory issue is fixed.

## Requested OpenAI investigation

Please assess local-execution safeguards around agent-generated commands: independent child-process RSS limits, termination of runaway children on interruption/application exit, and bounded error/object formatting before tool-output retention and desktop rendering. A display/output token cap alone cannot stop allocations performed inside an assertion formatter before output is emitted. Please avoid treating the user-reported aggregate peak as confirmed Codex-process usage without per-process evidence.

No private project source, customer content, addresses, screenshots, repository links, credentials, full transcripts, raw error graphs, or incident logs are included.

## Reporting channel

Official Codex troubleshooting documents GitHub issues and in-app feedback as supported reporting routes: [Feedback and logs](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/reference/troubleshooting#feedback-and-logs). No first-party feedback submission tool was exposed to this agent. A public issue will contain only this sanitized report. Submission status and verified issue URL will be recorded after the write succeeds.

### What issue are you seeing?

The ModRetro Chromatic plugin's browser preview can become permanently unresolved after an idle preview tab disappears. A subsequent close or ROM update fails with:

```text
Browser recording close acknowledgement is UNKNOWN.
```

The live preview reports `listening: true`, `closureState: unresolved`, and `players: []`, while retaining the identity of the previous ROM. Restarting Codex previously cleared the process-local state, but the problem returned during a later build update.

I reproduced the exact error in the shipped recording broker without ever starting a recording. The isolated test establishes the broker failure; the original browser tab-close event was not instrumented, so the complete browser event sequence remains an inference from the code and observed state.

### Environment

- macOS 27.0.1, arm64; Codex desktop's built-in browser.
- ModRetro Chromatic runtime: `1.0.33`.
- Installed distribution: `1.0.33+codex.distribution.66e8961d3ce30093ea3c9ee4`.
- Diagnosed and reproduced on 13 October 2026.
- Native ROM playback works on a Miyoo running Onion OS. The failure is in the browser-preview lifecycle.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

**Minimal deterministic reproducer, verified:** save the complete script below as `reproduce-recording-drain.mjs` and run:

```sh
node reproduce-recording-drain.mjs /absolute/path/to/modretro-chromatic
```

It imports the installed broker read-only, creates fresh isolated brokers and a disposable temporary project, and advances the broker's injected clock. It does not connect to the real preview, load a game ROM, edit the plugin cache, or alter user recordings or saves.

1. Register one idle recording view with `poll`; no recording is started.
2. Stop polling that view and advance the clock, modelling a vanished browser tab.
3. Call `drain` and advance beyond its 30-second deadline.
4. Observe the exact UNKNOWN error despite `busy === false` and no recording job.
5. Observe that the view and expired close barrier remain registered. An isolated retry fails again.

Control cases confirm that an original view's close acknowledgement, or explicit idle retirement before close, allows drain to complete. A new tab's acknowledgement does not release an abandoned old view. A human recording continues to block replacement.

<details>
<summary>Complete runnable reproducer (seven cases)</summary>

```javascript
// Isolated diagnostic for ModRetro Chromatic 1.0.33. Imports the installed
// broker read-only; never contacts or mutates a live preview, ROM, or save.
// Usage: node reproduce-recording-drain.mjs /absolute/path/to/plugin
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
import { mkdtemp, rm, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { resolve, join } from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';

assert.ok(process.argv[2], 'Supply the installed plugin directory.');
const { PreviewRecordingBroker } = await import(pathToFileURL(
  join(resolve(process.argv[2]), 'dist/web-preview-recording.js')));
const project = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), 'chromatic-preview-repro-'));
const results = [];
const idle = () => ({ generation: 0, available: true, visible: true,
  owner: null, phase: 'idle', pending: false });

async function check(name, run) {
  let mono = 0;
  const broker = new PreviewRecordingBroker(project, () => 1_800_000_000_000, 0, () => mono);
  try {
    await run(broker, ms => { mono += ms; });
    results.push({ name, passed: true });
    console.log(`PASS: ${name}`);
  } catch (error) {
    results.push({ name, passed: false, error: String(error) });
    console.error(`FAIL: ${name}: ${error}`);
    process.exitCode = 1;
  } finally {
    broker.dispose();
  }
}

try {
  await check('No registered views: close succeeds', async broker => {
    assert.equal(broker.busy, false);
    await broker.drain();
  });

  await check('Closed idle tab: UNKNOWN even though no recording ever existed', async (broker, advance) => {
    const view = randomUUID();
    broker.poll(view, idle());
    advance(60_000); // A vanished tab: its last poll was a minute ago.
    const closing = broker.drain();
    advance(30_001); // Advance the injected clock, not the live service.
    await assert.rejects(closing, /Browser recording close acknowledgement is UNKNOWN/);
    assert.equal(broker.busy, false);
    assert.equal(broker.job, undefined);
    assert.ok(broker.view(view), 'The abandoned idle view remains registered.');
    assert.equal(broker.closeUncertain, true);
    assert.equal(broker.remainingDrainMs, 0);
  });

  await check('Isolated retry: the expired close barrier is retained', async (broker, advance) => {
    broker.poll(randomUUID(), idle());
    const closing = broker.drain();
    const epoch = broker.closeState.id;
    advance(30_001);
    await assert.rejects(closing, /UNKNOWN/);
    await assert.rejects(broker.drain(), /UNKNOWN/);
    assert.equal(broker.closeState.id, epoch);
    assert.equal(broker.remainingDrainMs, 0);
  });

  await check('Original tab still polling: genuine close acknowledgement succeeds', async broker => {
    const view = randomUUID();
    broker.poll(view, idle());
    const closing = broker.drain();
    const reply = broker.poll(view, idle());
    assert.ok(reply.close);
    broker.poll(view, { ...idle(), closeEpoch: reply.close });
    await closing;
  });

  await check('Explicit idle-view retirement: later close succeeds', async broker => {
    const view = randomUUID();
    const retireId = randomUUID();
    broker.poll(view, idle());
    assert.deepEqual(broker.poll(view, { ...idle(), retireId }), { retired: retireId });
    assert.equal(broker.view(view), undefined);
    await broker.drain();
  });

  await check('New tab acknowledges but old tab vanished: close still fails', async (broker, advance) => {
    const oldView = randomUUID();
    const newView = randomUUID();
    broker.poll(oldView, idle());
    advance(60_000);
    broker.poll(newView, idle());
    const closing = broker.drain();
    const reply = broker.poll(newView, idle());
    broker.poll(newView, { ...idle(), closeEpoch: reply.close });
    advance(30_001);
    await assert.rejects(closing, /UNKNOWN/);
    assert.ok(broker.view(oldView));
  });

  await check('Human recording: replacement is refused and recording preserved', async broker => {
    const view = randomUUID();
    const human = { ...idle(), owner: 'human', phase: 'recording', pending: true };
    broker.poll(view, human);
    const closing = broker.drain();
    const reply = broker.poll(view, human);
    broker.poll(view, { ...human, closeEpoch: reply.close });
    await assert.rejects(closing, /A person is recording or saving/);
    assert.equal(broker.view(view).owner, 'human');
    assert.equal(broker.admission, true);
    assert.equal(broker.closeState, undefined);
  });

  await writeFile(new URL('./reproduction-results.json', import.meta.url),
    JSON.stringify({ testedAt: new Date().toISOString(), isolated: true,
      pluginVersion: '1.0.33', results }, null, 2) + '\n');
} finally {
  await rm(project, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

</details>

<details>
<summary>Actual test results</summary>

```json
{
  "testedAt": "2026-10-13T16:02:24.842Z",
  "isolated": true,
  "pluginVersion": "1.0.33",
  "results": [
    {
      "name": "No registered views: close succeeds",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "Closed idle tab: UNKNOWN even though no recording ever existed",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "Isolated retry: the expired close barrier is retained",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "Original tab still polling: genuine close acknowledgement succeeds",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "Explicit idle-view retirement: later close succeeds",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "New tab acknowledges but old tab vanished: close still fails",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "name": "Human recording: replacement is refused and recording preserved",
      "passed": true
    }
  ]
}
```

</details>

All seven assertions passed. A passing assertion for a failure case means it reproduced the defect, not that the defect was fixed.

**Browser scenario to verify end to end:** open an official web preview, allow the idle tab to register, close or navigate away from that tab, then request a build replacement or `web_preview_close`. The local live failure and code path match this scenario, but the isolated reproducer above is the controlled test evidence.

### What is the expected behavior?

Closing an idle browser tab should not permanently prevent preview closure or future ROM updates. The plugin should retire the view through a supported lifecycle flow, or provide a supported recovery path for abandoned views, while preserving real recordings and unfinished saves.

### Additional information

Relevant locations in the installed distribution:

- `dist/web-annotations/bridge.js:500`: `pagehide` disconnects player control.
- `dist/web-annotations/remote.js:173`: disconnect stops polling and aborts the outstanding request without retiring the recording view. The explicit retirement handshake at line 113 is bound to the player's Restart action.
- `dist/web-preview-recording.js:176`: polls register views; normal idle views do not expire. Explicit retirement removes a known idle view.
- `dist/web-preview-recording.js:367`: drain requires every registered view to acknowledge a new close epoch. Its expired deadline remains after failure.
- `dist/web-preview-player.js:93`: the displayed player list independently filters stale views, explaining why status can show `players: []` while recording closure remains blocked.
- `dist/web-preview.js:1161`: build replacement drains the old broker before updating the ROM identity; failure retains the old identity and marks closure unresolved.

Simply dropping every stale, last-known-idle entry would miss a possible human recording started after its last poll. A fix should cover normal tab closure, navigation, reload, disconnection, repeated build replacement, active recordings and pending saves.

**Workaround evidence:** a Codex restart cleared an earlier stuck preview. Keeping the original view connected until close is acknowledged passes the broker control case; that prevention sequence still needs an end-to-end browser check after recovery.

The package's advertised repository, `OpenAI-Partners/ModRetro-Chromatic-Plugin-for-Codex`, returned HTTP 404 to a public fetch, so I am reporting this integration defect here. If another tracker is appropriate, please point me to it.

Related but distinct: #51764 concerns a native-emulator recording lease and was closed after moving files out of iCloud-synchronised storage. This report concerns a browser view acknowledgement, reproduced in memory without any recording job.

No private session IDs, capability URLs, local account paths, raw conversation logs or game files are included.
